;;; A hypothesis whose instance is `eq` is proposed, and a commitment stores
;;; it — regression `hrule-eq`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`); no commitment changes `eq` or `neq`
;;; (Q-M1e2.26).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts `(p a)` and `(p b)`, and a rule that supposes `(?R ?x ?y)` for
;;; every two `p` facts, its activator `(h eq)` binding `?R` to `eq`. The
;;; grammar refuses `(open (eq ?x ?y))` written out: `eq` heads no fact.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A commitment states facts a state stores, and a state stores no `eq`, whose
;;; facts are computed: a hypothesis's `eq` or `neq` instance is none
;;; (Q-M1f.32). H is empty, and R is {∅}.
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The rule proposed `(eq a a)`, `(eq a b)`, `(eq b a)` and `(eq b b)`, each
;;; committed as a stored fact, the false `(eq a b)` among them, so R was
;;; sixteen states, exit 0. The same rule given `neq` proposed the four `neq`
;;; facts, whose state files do not load, a fact line `(neq …)` being a parse
;;; error; this witness is the `eq` one so that its state files load
;;; (`answer_round_trip.rs`).

(relation p Node :why "{?3} is a node")
(p a)
(p b)
(rule h (?R)
  :match (and (p ?x) (p ?y))
  :assert (open (?R ?x ?y))
  :why "suppose {?x} and {?y} stand in {?R}")
(h eq)
